 From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genitive_case

In Old English, a common singular genitive ending was -es. The 18th 
century explanation that the apostrophe might replace a genitive 
pronoun, as in "the king's horse" being a shortened form of 'the king, 
his horse', is erroneous. Rather, the apostrophe is replacing the 'e' 
from the Old English morphology.


